1. `unordered maps` : unordered maps has all same functions(expect the function which we 
    can use for maps to do tasks related to sorting they are in maps but no in unordered_maps)
    which we used in maps and also the way to access and insert any key-value pair . 
	1. `syntax ` :` unordered_map<int,string> n;` n is unordered map of int-string type.
	2. `Order` : They are neither the order in which we inserted value nor they are sorted(neither
	ascending or descending order) basically whenever we enter any key-value pair in it this use 
	internally hash table and then convert that key to a hash number and then insert accordingly.
	3. `time complexity` : average time complexity of insertion and access is O(1) for one time. 
	4. `Chosing between` : whenever order doesn't matter in answer then use unordered maps because 
	they take less time and hence more efficient whenever order doesn't matter. Also make sure when 
	it comes to complex data types like pair or something then you can use unordered maps only that 
	has their inbuilt hash function to calculate their hash values otherwise you can't use unordered 
	maps for them(like for pairs you can't use undered maps) but maps are valid for almost all data 
	types because they take insertion on the basis of comparison and alsmost all data types can be 
	compared. Basically basic data types have their own built in hash functions to calculate internally
	like long long , int , strings , etc but sets, vectors , pairs , etc don't have so we can't use 
	unordered maps for them. Means whenever we are wanted to use key as a primitive data only when use 
	unordered maps and if key is complex data type then maps . 
	
	
2. `multi maps` : These are almost never used in competitive programming the key difference is that you can 
    insert unique keys only in maps but you can even insert duplicate keys in multi maps and even if you wanted
    to store multi values corresponding to any key then we can create vector of that like `map<int,vector<string>> n;`
    now we can store multiples values for a key . 
    
    
3. `even ∗ even = even;even ∗ odd = even;odd ∗ even = even;odd ∗ odd = odd.` : any number can be formed in which condtition .


4. `bitwise operators` : these are the operations we can do on any integers or character data types only because characters 
    has a ascii table which bitwise operator can internally refer to do operations . We can use & , | , ^ , >> , << .
	1. `bitwise and (&)` : whenever use this on any two operands(whether they are integers or character) our compiler first 
	convert them in form of 0 and 1 and equal their size for the shortest one it will add 0's in the prefix to make digits 
	equal to bigger operand then this will compare bit by bit and if both bits are 1 then return 1 and if both bits are 
	different (or both bits are 0) then this will return 0 and we consider 1 or any positive integer as true and 0 as false. 
	2. `bitwise or (|)` : our compiler will convert integer or char type in binary form and then compare bit by bit if either
	one from them is 1 then it will return 1 . 
	3. `bitwise xor` : First convert in binary form and then compare each bit by bit and if bits are same then this will return
	0 otherwise 1.
	4. `bitwise right shift >> `: this is used in cp for division of any number by powers of 2 as 10 >> 1 this divide 10 by 
	1 by shifting each bit to right side and this is much faster then standard 10/2 and after >> write the power for that 
	power's you want as exponent and 2 as a base.  Like 10 >> 1 is (10/ 2 raise to 1) generalisation is n >> k then (n/ 2 
	raise to k)
	5. `bitwise left shift << ` : this is used in cp for fast multiplication of any number with 2 as base and power k as 
	`n << k ` this is n* (2 raise to k) and this is faster then n * (pow(2,k)) 
	6. `Some useful mathematical implementations for cp ` : 
		1. `checking whether a number is built of only 2 and its powers means like 8 is 2 raise to 3` : (n& (n-1)) and 
		if this return 0 then our number is built solely from 2 and its powers . 
